ademy-id "q-129906" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/1b1663fc5459119be23806dece661979722ac2ef507add83f3e71d7799246130.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/bc6d7409dee2b5935d03839d32fc3aad23f08f1dd2c65eae496df8f995960b23.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III are equals to $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-129906/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-129906"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80540/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "8940604e-d5d7-51b1-bf55-202f74463393" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80540/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "966cb8c3-1c2c-5d45-be48-9bbcd486575f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80540/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "5578d825-83d1-59ed-9be0-06614749d0cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80540/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5d47a83f-794a-5a9a-996e-389a39289ac8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80540/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "ff5f4159-a261-5c71-8a06-965ea4517477" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80540/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "81609633-6fc6-5788-a39c-1839631bbfe4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80540/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-80540/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-80540/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-80540/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-80540/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-80540/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80540" :question/id #uuid "6eb5a26d-0c9a-5754-8686-78fa12fef675" :question/math-academy-id "q-80540" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/506a298041a875549ec60c5cf99ebdbeb5ca4b5717037083fa6ca196fce27279.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ \\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/d2b5c55b15f594680e8f299899d690cd1d92baa1ebef31ce5c79af52afec631e.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function II. Since $D∖R$ is empty, the function $f(x,y)$ will be identical to zero for all points in $R,$ including $D.$ But $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ is not zero, for example, at $(x,y)=(0,2)\\in D.$\n\n- Functions I and III equals $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80540/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80540"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-80425"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/d09a23a1ae9f737c7f19ba83d6d20c3fbcea3c7deb71fa307264c90afabc6580.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/1620acdcfd3783df478322ebe5e81cb274d908db12d9c8bdc701fe21070b828f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6997"] :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $x^2+y^2=9.$ If \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/77c124998f7dba480ae7a68547a72d35b86c3946e7efbf51072896051c9aeca6.png) \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-4,4] \\times [-4,4]$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-3,3] \\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/3aab7a115fe72d3796be28c66f4ce9ae2c15dc56e09efd98e5ad9a45edebd704.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/676f189bafbe67e0d7c0562ae83921fe9ea27d0123ff81979cd64baf55d1dcc6.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6995"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/1191de029d927394dcc587f9c432a2d7cf4cec136e6d6ebbe62799de7b4f9b3e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,1] \\times [0,1],$ and\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D |x-y| \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ |x-y|, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/d9ca0bfcfde5839033279cb09f8ccece7c59a5b8f8b24eaae3697fd5358dc6b3.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $|x-y|$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D |x-y| \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\\backslash D.$ For example, $(x,y) = (1,0) \\in R\\,\\backslash\\, D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III equal $|x-y|$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
